{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"b727394e1dca408e","type":"text","text":"Ashk'lan religion is based upon the Ancient Greek one -- people do not supplicate to gods and instead treat their relationship as a transaction.\nThe King had since long looked to turn the Priests away from their gods entirely, and worship only himself. The Priests may have made an appearance of complying, deep inside, they foster the idea of taking revenge -- especially after the King took away some of their land to give to his nobles.","x":-300,"y":100,"width":663,"height":187},
		{"id":"e7d5d2707e9fa4ac","x":468,"y":359,"width":852,"height":561,"type":"text","text":"Askelani Priests worship ancient gods older than the Golden City itself, come from the blessed Desert. Their cult in Askelan had persisted past the death of these gods at the hands of the Janins, although their spirit was made mellower and their names twisted. In the City's decadence, the Gods, too, are second to the _people_ who worship them, if it may be called that. \n\nOf the older Gods most respected in Askelan, there are Cyr, Atash and Balhadad; originally wild elemental personifications, their faces were since humanized. Only a little of their former might remains in the Priests' everburning altars; they tame it and bring it to battle. Cyr, the owl-faced god of knowledge, scribes, distinction through talent, and discipline has a large temple in the eastern end of Askelan, where the Astromancers also dwell. These two cults used to be in close contact, but have since drifted apart; what remains is an uneasy alliance, based on nothing but propriety. In battle, Priests of Cyr, the Cyrodes, can weaken enemies by appealing to the water within their brains to directly reduce their Damage. There are both men and women among Cyrodes, characterized by chaste demeanors and many rules binding them. One such is aversion to divine images of any sort. \n\nAtash, the fiery god, oversees justice, craftsmen and especially smiths. It is his priests that people of Askelan come to when there is a contention among them; it is said that Atash can detect sinners who step into his holy flames. The duty of judges and executioners was given to these priests by the King of old, who still feared the gods; today, the King's Law slowly takes over, leaving the Priests, Atarites, by the wayside. Askelani say that Atash was the father of the three Forge Patrons who brought mastery over metals to Anghu, though Stoneseekers instead place Atash as their son. It is known that the Guilds have taken many of their tenets from Atarite lore, though today Priests and Guilds do not get along anymore."},
		{"id":"b6701f09e5bba05d","x":491,"y":989,"width":949,"height":371,"type":"text","text":"Atarites are mostly men. Their temple is located in the upper city, in a prestigious district, bearing down upon all who come to seek their cases heard. Their magic is able to control fire, though in a different way than that of the Pyrecallers; they wield blazing materials of the First Heaven, which they lob into enemy ranks to slowly, but inexorably, burn down their resistance.\n\nLastly, the once-god of thunder Balhadad, the father of Salkhi's Wanjin, is extant in Askelan in a gelded form; his cult chose first to defer to the King's Law, and became changed in its image. Balhadad is the god of soldiers and nobles, who is the ground of any distinguished blood and its privilege. Where Atash helps dole out the punishment unto sinners, Balhadad oversees capturing them and bringing them before the Law. He is, in a way of speaking, the thunder one hears before the King's forces fall upon them. This is why the majority of policing around Ask'lan is done by Balhadad's battle monks rather than King's Men themselves. In a less threatening display, Balhadad's priests also oversee contracts and promises, be that in trade or marriage. Balhadad's priests wield maces in battle and enable improved control over status buffs, the hallmark of the City's forces. For their deference to the King, they are allowed a temple on the palace grounds, otherwise off-limits to the populace. Anyone can become a member of Balhadad's forces, though not everyone is allowed to rise through the ranks higher than their blood allows them to."},
		{"id":"e01e8f8eb9c24f2a","x":-353,"y":455,"width":753,"height":665,"type":"text","text":"1. Sexton (Balhadad) (Armored, with shield and a mace) Purity (immune to debuffs), nightslayer. \n2. Kratios (Balhadad) (In a triumphant pose, with a scepter with a red jewel. Name means 'vigorous') Cast: grant all allies 1 of each buff this creature has. \n3. Endymion (Balhadad) (Armored, with a lightning crown. Name means 'guilt') Cast: steal all buffs from the target. \n4. Synesis (Balhadad) -- Ritual (A hand surrounded by lightning aura. Name means 'conscience') Double all buffs on the target creature. \n5. Man's Resilience (Balhadad) -- Equipment (A mace with a head that looks like an empty glass vessel) On turn: for each debuff, bolster 0/+1. \n6. Pyromancer (Atash) (Wears a robe that seems to be burning all over. Means 'Fire Diviner' lol) Takes no damage from Burn. On turn: gain 1 Burn. If Burn = this creature's Health, it dies. Cast: Gain 1 Burn. Inflict damage on target equal to this creature's Burn. \n7. Calcas (Atash) (In a battle-stance. Holds a staff in one hand, and a fireball in the other) Cast: burns away 1 Mana from the enemy, dealing 1 damage to the enemy player and corroding self by 1. \n8. Blazing Cup (Atash) (A small altar with a fire in it) Structure. On turn: corrode all enemies 1. \n9. Crysis (Atash) -- Ritual (Name means 'trial') Inflict both Player and Opponent with Crysis. Until the end of the battle, both lose 1 health at the start of their turns and their Max Mana no longer increases.\n10. Ogunon (Atash) -- Equipment (A flanged mace with its head designed as tongues of flame. Named after the Yoruba god Ogun, the other inspiration for Atash here) Takes no damage from Burn. Deal extra damage equal to burn on self and the target.\n11. Mielopath (Cyr) (A priest in a navy robe and a winged crown, with one hand reaching forward) Cast: weaken enemy Damage by 1 until next turn. Inflict Compromised. For each stack of Compromised, all weaken effects above 0 are stronger."}
	],
	"edges":[]
}